<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jerónimos Monastery</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jerónimos Monastery is a monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> built from the early sixteenth century in the ornate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manueline-style">Manueline style</a>. Its construction was funded by revenues from the spice trade. It is a symbol of the wealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> in the age of discovery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeronimos-monastery.jpg" alt="Jerónimos Monastery" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Holger Uwe Schmitt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1502_legte_K%C3%B6nig_Manuel_I._den_Grundstein_f%C3%BCr_das_Hieronymus_Kloster_in_Lissabon._06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Jerónimos Monastery, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a> in the parish of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belem">Belém</a>, replaced a church of Santa Maria de Belém, where monks of the Order of Christ assisted seafarers. Manuel I ordered the new building at the cortes of Montemor o Velho in 1495 as a final resting place for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a>, and in 1496 he petitioned the Holy See for permission to build the monastery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> and his men spent the night in prayer at the old church before leaving for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1497.</p><p>Construction began on 6 January 1501 and took a century. It was funded by the Vintena da Pimenta, a 5 percent tax on commerce from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the Orient, equal to 70 kilograms of gold a year. Manuel I chose Hieronymite monks to pray for his soul and to give spiritual help to navigators leaving from Restelo, which they did until 1833.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The monastery is among the most prominent examples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manueline-style">Manueline style</a>, a richly ornate architecture carved in limestone with maritime themes and objects found on naval expeditions. Diogo de Boitaca drew the plans, and the Spaniard Juan de Castillo took charge around 1517, moving gradually towards the Spanish Plateresque. Building halted when Manuel I died in 1521. Diogo de Torralva resumed work in 1550 and added the main chapel and choir, and Jérôme de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> continued from 1571.</p><p>The church is a hall church with a single-span ribbed vault over a nave 19 metres wide and a transept vault without piers, which seems to float in the air. Six slender octagonal columns, 25 metres high, are carved with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> decoration. The south portal, designed by Castillo, is 32 metres high, while Nicolau Chanterene built the western portal in 1517. The cloister, 55 by 55 metres, carries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manueline-style">Manueline</a> motifs with nautical elements and symbols of Portugal's growing world power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Work stopped in 1580 with the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal, when the building of the Escorial drained funds. In 1604 Philip of Spain made it a royal funerary monument. The monastery withstood the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> earthquake of 1755 with little damage, but on 28 December 1833 it was secularised and transferred to the Real Casa Pia de Lisboa. Restoration began after 1860.</p><p>The church holds the tombs of kings Manuel I and John III, and of the explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-camoes">Luís de Camões</a>, whose remains were moved to carved tombs in 1880. The monastery and the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tower-of-belem">Tower of Belém</a> were classified as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1983, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> was signed there on 13 December 2007. Its west wing houses the Maritime Museum, inaugurated in 1963.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jerónimos Monastery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Portuguese culture produced the Manueline style of the <b>Jerónimos Monastery</b> and the Tower of Belém, the epic Os Lusíadas of Luís de Camões, published in 1572, and advances in navigation and cartography.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The spice trade was enormously profitable in the decades around 1500–1550: pepper, cinnamon, cloves and nutmeg generated revenues that made Lisbon one of Europe’s wealthiest cities and funded the Manueline building programme, including the <b>Jerónimos Monastery</b> and the Tower of Belém.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
